course: history
lesson: 18
title: The Maya
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Maya lived in the rainforests and hills of southern Mexico and Central America and built great stone cities like Tikal and Palenque, at their height between about 1,700 and 1,100 years ago.
explain: They had a full writing system, brilliant maths with their own zero, and calendars that tracked the sun, the moon and Venus. Scholars have been able to read most of their writing only since the 1970s.
explain: Think of a phone with the battery gone flat: everything is still inside, but you cannot read it. That is what happened when Spanish priests burned Maya books; only four survive, and a huge amount of knowledge was lost for ever.
explain: Many southern cities were abandoned around 1,100 years ago, probably because of droughts, wars and too many people for the land. But the Maya did not vanish: millions of Maya people live in the same lands today and speak Mayan languages.
fact: The Maya lived in the rainforests and hills of southern Mexico and Central America and built great cities between about 1,700 and 1,100 years ago.
fact: Millions of Maya people still live in the same lands today and speak Mayan languages; the Maya did not disappear.
fact: Maya cities like Tikal and Palenque had tall stone pyramids with temples on top, palaces and plazas, and some are still being discovered with lasers fired from aeroplanes.
fact: The Maya had a full writing system of glyphs, carved on stone and painted in folding books made of bark, and it has mostly been deciphered since the 1970s.
fact: Spanish priests burned many Maya books, thinking them dangerous, and only four are known to survive.
fact: The Maya counted in twenties and had their own sign for zero, a shell, invented separately from India.
fact: The Maya had very accurate calendars that tracked the sun, the moon and Venus, which helped them plan farming and festivals.
fact: The Maya calendar did not predict the end of the world in 2012; one long cycle simply ended and a new one began.
fact: The Maya played a ball game with a heavy rubber ball, hitting it with their hips through stone rings high on a wall, and the game had religious meaning.
fact: Rubber was a Native American discovery, made from the sap of trees.
fact: The Maya and Aztecs made a bitter, frothy chocolate drink from cacao beans, with no sugar and sometimes with chilli, and cacao beans were even used as money.
fact: Around 1,100 years ago many southern Maya cities were abandoned, probably because of long droughts, wars and too many people for the land.
fact: Later Maya cities in the north, like Chichen Itza, kept going, and at the spring and autumn equinox a shadow snake seems to slide down the stairs of its great pyramid.
exercise: Where did the Maya live? | Southern Mexico and Central America | Peru and Chile, high up in the Andes mountains | Egypt and Sudan | Canada and Alaska
exercise: When were the great Maya cities at their height? | About 1,700 to 1,100 years ago | About 5,000 years ago, before the pyramids of Egypt | About 100 years ago | About 50,000 years ago
exercise: Which is a Maya city? | Tikal | Tenochtitlan | Cusco in Peru | Cahokia
exercise: What stood on top of Maya pyramids? | Temples | Tombs of pharaohs | Lighthouses | Windmills
exercise: How are new Maya cities being found today? | With lasers fired from aeroplanes | By digging up every single field by hand with spades | By asking robots on the Moon | They are not, all are found
exercise: What did the Maya make their folding books from? | Bark | Clay | Silk | Papyrus
exercise: Since when has most Maya writing been read? | Since the 1970s | Since 1492 | Since the Romans | It has never been read
exercise: How many Maya books are known to survive? | Four | Four hundred | Forty thousand | None
exercise: Who burned many Maya books? | Spanish priests | The Aztecs | Vikings | The Maya kings themselves
exercise: The Maya counted in groups of what? | Twenty | Sixty | Ten | Twelve
exercise: What sign did the Maya use for zero? | A shell | A dot | A circle with a cross | A star
exercise: Which planet did Maya calendars track? | Venus | Neptune | Saturn's rings | Distant Pluto
exercise: How did players hit the ball in the Maya ball game? | With their hips | With wooden bats | With their heads only | With their feet like football
exercise: What was the Maya ball made of? | Rubber | Leather | Plastic | Carved wood
exercise: What did the Maya make from cacao beans? | A bitter, frothy drink | Sweet milk chocolate bars with sugar | Bread | Paint for walls
exercise: Why were many southern Maya cities abandoned? | Probably droughts, wars and too many people for the land | A single volcano in one day | The Spanish attacked them all in 900 | Everyone moved to Europe
exercise: What can you see at Chichen Itza at the equinox? | A shadow snake on the pyramid stairs | A golden light that shines from the very top | Snow on the pyramid | A giant eclipse of the sun every single year
test: my uncle says the maya predicted the end of the world in 2012. did they? | No, a long calendar cycle just ended and a new one began | Yes, and it nearly happened | Yes, it was moved to 2050 | No, they said 3000
test: A documentary says the Maya vanished without a trace. What is wrong? | Millions of Maya people still live there today | Nothing, they are all gone and nobody speaks Maya | They moved to the Moon | They became Vikings
test: The Maya and the Indians both had zero. Did one copy the other? | No, they invented it separately | Yes, India copied the Maya after sailors visited | Yes, the Maya copied India | No, the Romans gave it to both
test: Imagine the Maya could pay for a hat with cacao beans. What were beans being used as? | Money | Medicine | Bricks | Toys for babies
test: If the Spanish had not burned the books, what would we probably know? | Much more about Maya history and beliefs | Less about the Maya than we know now | Nothing new | The exact date the world will end, written in the books
test: Why might a farmer care about an accurate calendar? | To know when to plant and harvest | To know exactly when the next bus comes past the farm | To count his birthdays | To pay his phone bill
test: Your chocolate bar is sweet; Maya chocolate was not. What was different? | It was bitter, frothy, sometimes with chilli, and had no sugar | It was frozen like ice cream | It was made from milk only | It was the same as ours
test: Long droughts hit a big city with too many people. What lesson does the Maya story teach? | Look after the land and water | Build taller pyramids and pray harder to the rain god | Never play ball games | Burn more books
test: Where does the rubber for the first bouncy balls come from? | Tree sap, discovered by Native Americans | Oil wells in Europe dug in the Middle Ages | Silkworms in China | Volcano rocks
test: Why was the shadow snake at Chichen Itza clever? | It showed how well the Maya knew the sun's movements | It was painted on every day | It was a real snake | It was made by Spanish priests
test: Mate says the Maya could only draw pictures, not write. Is he right? | No, they had a full writing system of glyphs | Yes, they had no writing at all, only pictures | Yes, only cave paintings | No, they wrote everything in Latin
exam: Identify the region inhabited by the Maya civilisation. | Southern Mexico and Central America | The Andes of Peru | The Great Plains of North America, beside the Mississippi | The valley of the Nile
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Maya calendar and the year 2012. | A long cycle concluded; no end of the world was predicted | The end of the world was explicitly foretold | The calendar terminated in 1492 | The calendar was devised by the Spaniards
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Maya conception of zero? | It was devised independently and represented by a shell | It was borrowed from India by sea | It was introduced by Spanish priests | It was unknown to them
exam: Ascertain the number of Maya books known to have survived their destruction by Spanish clergy. | Four | Four hundred | Forty | None
